# house
## The Marigny
$ do chart("neworleans.haven")
$ _lead = business_lead()
$ _watch = gift_holder("strength")
?if _watch == "" or _watch == _lead
  $ _watch = ""
  ?if cast(2) != ""
    $ _watch = cast(2)
  ?elif cast(1) != ""
    $ _watch = cast(1)
  ?end
?end
?if coven_size() > 0 and b4.circle != "stay"
  The coven is awake, and in the long room, and not at all asleep. It is a thing that a house knows, in the grain of the floor, on the night before a country goes to war: it is a very small thing, and it is the whole of the house.
  ?if cast_count() >= 1
    {name(cast(1))} has laid out the ledgers on the dining table in the order of their danger.
  ?end
  ?if cast_count() >= 2
    {name(cast(2))} has opened a map.
  ?end
  ?if cast_count() >= 3
    {name(cast(3))} is sitting in the window, with {pg(cast(3), "his", "her", "their")} chin on {pg(cast(3), "his", "her", "their")} hand, watching the street.
  ?end
  ?if b4.errand == "scout" and b4.errand_who != "" and b4.errand_who != 0 and in_circle(b4.errand_who) and member(b4.errand_who, "status") == "active" and not on_mission(b4.errand_who)
    {name(b4.errand_who)} has come home. {pg(b4.errand_who, "He", "She", "They")} {pg(b4.errand_who, "says", "says", "say")}, without looking up from the map, that the river houses have eleven roads out of the city, and that three of them are watched, and that two of the watchers are paid by the same man, who is not a vampire and is a good deal more frightening. A finger goes down on the paper. It is a very exact map. It has the sleep habits of every dog on the Bayou Road in the margin.
  ?elif b4.errand == "protect" and b4.errand_who != "" and b4.errand_who != 0 and in_circle(b4.errand_who) and member(b4.errand_who, "status") == "active" and not on_mission(b4.errand_who)
    {name(b4.errand_who)} has been back for two nights, and has not said very much about it. {pg(b4.errand_who, "He", "She", "They")} {pg(b4.errand_who, "says", "says", "say")} only that Dr. Armand has a very good lock, and a very bad habit of not using it, and that {pg(b4.errand_who, "he", "she", "they")} {pg(b4.errand_who, "has", "has", "have")} been told, with great firmness, not to sit on his step. It was sat on anyway. It is plain, from the way that it is said, that {pg(b4.errand_who, "he", "she", "they")} {pg(b4.errand_who, "has", "has", "have")} begun to be fond of a person for whom there is no intention of admitting it.
  ?end
  There is, in the room, the thing that has been in every war house in the world: a plan. They are going to need more than a good lock. They have been talking, in your absence, in the way that a family does when the head of it is out, and they have come to a number of conclusions, none of which is at all what you expected.
?elif coven_size() > 0
  The house is quiet and dark. They are in a far country, and you find that you have set a place, in your head, for each of them at the long table. It is not a thing that a man can do for very long.
?else
  The house is quiet. You walk through the rooms, in the dark, with a candle, and count the doors. It is a thing that you have done in a good many houses. You find that you are looking, in this one, for the places where somebody could come in.
?end
:the_works
* Open a way out under the carriage house, to the old drain of the Marigny ?if can_acquire("escape_tunnels")
  $ do acquire("escape_tunnels")
  $ secrecy += 1
  You have it done by two Irish diggers from the canal, who ask no questions and are paid for their lack of them, and who work in a lantern's light under the cooperage floor for three nights, and come up at the end of the third with a perfectly dry brick passage that runs under two lots and ends in a crypt of the St. Roch cemetery. They do not look at the walls. They go out into the daylight and, you believe, to a very large drink.
* Set up a few eyes in the city: a clerk at the telegraph, a man at the Custom House, a widow with a good window ?if can_acquire("informants")
  $ do acquire("informants")
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  It costs a good deal less than you would have thought, and is worth a good deal more. There is always, in a city, a person who has seen a thing and not been asked. You find three in the first week, and a fourth is brought to you, a little grudgingly, by one of the family, who has been trying very hard not to enjoy it.
* Take a second house, a plain one, on the Bayou Road, as a place to be found in ?if can_acquire("safehouses")
  $ do acquire("safehouses")
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ do earn(-2)
  It is a dull little house, with a green door and a cistern, and a woman who comes in by the day to air it. It has no mirror, no portrait, no book, no sign that anyone has ever lived in it. It is an excellent place, as a former Roman remarks, to be mistaken for somebody else.
* Set {name(_watch)} to the watch of the Marigny house ?if _watch != "" and b4.circle != "stay" and task(_watch) != "guard"
  $ do coven_task(_watch, "guard")
  {name(_watch)} says that it has been waiting to be asked, which is a thing that is said about everything, and is a good deal more often true than anyone has liked to say. Out into the courtyard {pg(_watch, "he", "she", "they")} {pg(_watch, "goes", "goes", "go")} at once, and walks the wall, and comes back in with a very long, very sharp, very unnecessary knife. You do not ask where it was found.
* Ask {name(_lead)} to carry the firm's paper through the war ?if _lead != "" and b4.circle != "stay" and task(_lead) != "business"
  $ do coven_task(_lead, "business")
  {name(_lead)} takes it as every ledger that has ever been given has been taken, with a very small, very tidy nod, and a pencil. A war, {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} {pg(_lead, "says", "says", "say")}, is the best time in the world to be quiet with a good abacus, and money has been made in a great many wars by someone who has hated every one. You have the impression, without being told, that it is a thing not said aloud for a very long time.
* That will do for tonight
  -> :house_done
-> :the_works
:house_done
<~

# dawn
## The gun
It comes at nine, from the battery at the Place d'Armes, a single flat heavy report, and then another, and another. They are firing a salute. It takes you a moment, at the window, to count. It is twenty one. They are firing it for the state.
It has been done. The telegraph from Baton Rouge has said the one word, which is *Out*. Within an hour, there are flags from every window in the Rue Royale, the blue flag with the single star, and the pelican, and in the Place d'Armes a band is playing something that is almost a waltz, and men are embracing in the street. It is a sunny, cold, bright morning. It is the loveliest day that the winter has had.
?if b4.levy == "gave"
  There is a notice on the door of the counting house, chalked by a child: *Honor to the House of Colline.* Aurélien has not wiped it off. He is standing by it, with his hat in his hand, and a face that you would give a good deal not to have seen.
?elif b4.levy == "lent"
  The contract is on the desk, in a clerk's round hand, with a penciled addition in the margin. The state is a good customer. It is a great deal better than a patron. You feel that you are the only man in New Orleans this morning who is going to be paid at six per cent, and you have the odd sensation of having been shrewd on the wrong side of a very large door.
?else
  There is no chalk on the door this morning, and none, you notice, on the doors of the Valère house. There is a small cold silence all round them, as there is round a house in which somebody is ill. A man in a blue shirt, going by with his pelican, has stopped and is looking up at the sign. He looks for some time. Then he spits, quite carefully, in the gutter, and goes on.
?end
Aurélien has brought you the paper. It is, as it has been all winter, a very good paper. It does not, this morning, say anything much. It prints the vote, and the salute, and the price of sugar. You turn to the last page, where there is a column of small notices, and you find, in the third line from the foot, between an advertisement for a lost cow and another for a very superior mattress, a short plain sentence in French that was not there in the first edition. *Persons of the old acquaintance of Madame L. of the Bayou are requested to call.*
?if b4.shelter == "hid"
  Someone, in your own counting house, has paid for it. You do not ask who.
?elif b4.shelter == "sent"
  It is the girl's hand, you realize, in the choice of the words. She has learned the trick of the small notice very quickly, in a very short time, from a lake boat.
?else
  It is a fine sentence. It has no name in it, and no accusation, and a plain long courtesy that goes on and on like a bell in a field. It is a notice that you could not have written, and could not have refused to read.
?end
There is a letter on the hall table, with a Havana postmark, that has been at the post for a month. It is from Crane. It is very short.
> You will, I hope, forgive me for observing that the South is a good deal less interesting at the end of a war than at the beginning of one. There are others of our acquaintance in this city who will tell you otherwise. They will tell you a great many things. I should be very glad to hear which of them you believed. H. C.
You read it twice, and put it in the stove, and then, quite deliberately, take it out again, and put it in your pocket. It is not a good idea. It is a thing that you are going to do.
$ do rest()
